TAM-CLAM - THE TAMLINUX SHELL

tam-clam is our own command shell. It is built fresh, from the ground up,
and it builds on the best ideas and the best code of bash, zsh, fish,
dash, ksh, mksh, Oils and every other shell we can find. Where another
shell's code works well, we take it and refactor it into tam-clam's own
shape. Where it doesn't, we leave it out. tam-clam is not a fork or a
clone of any of them.

The goal is a bullet-proof shell: one that never crashes, never corrupts
its history, never runs a command differently from what you typed, and
tells you clearly what went wrong and where.

Follows the tam-idol 0.2.0 standard.


WHAT IT WILL DO

  Run POSIX shell scripts exactly. The language core is the POSIX shell
  language, so scripts written for /bin/sh, dash or bash --posix run
  unchanged, and tam-clam can one day be /bin/sh.

  Add the bash and zsh features people really use: arrays, [[ ]], local,
  process substitution, a strong history and programmable completion.

  Be pleasant to type in, like fish: syntax highlighting as you type,
  suggestions from history, abbreviations, and completions for common
  commands, all in our own line editor.

  Offer safer modes for new scripts, like Oils: errors that are never
  silently ignored, no surprise word splitting, and messages that point at
  the exact line and column. They are opt-in, so POSIX scripts keep
  running as written.

  Until a feature lands, tam-clam refuses it with a clear message instead
  of running the command some other way. Version 0.0.1, for example, runs
  simple commands and says "pipelines are not supported yet" for a pipe.


HOW IT IS BUILT

  - Small modules with one job each, and explicit state: every function is
    handed the shell it works on, and the only global state is the flags
    signal handlers set.
  - Memory for each command comes from an arena and is freed in one step
    when the command finishes. Every allocation is checked, and input has
    size and depth limits, so a hostile script cannot exhaust the machine.
  - A hand-written recursive-descent parser follows the POSIX grammar. No
    yacc, no generated tables.
  - Every dependency has to earn its place. The goal is the C library and
    libtam, and nothing else: no readline, no ncurses, no terminfo, no
    pcre. The line editor is our own, built on libtam's terminal and key
    modules.
  - Tested three ways: unit tests for each module, script tests compared
    against a reference shell (dash, or bash --posix), and builds with
    the address and undefined-behaviour sanitizers.
  - Plain Make. There is no other build system.

  arch/principles.md has the full set of principles.


BUILDING

  Needs: a C11 compiler, make, and libtam installed under LIBTAM_PREFIX
  (default $HOME/.local).

    make              build build/tam-clam
    make test         unit tests, then the script tests
    make sanitize     the tests again, built with ASan and UBSan
    make install      install to PREFIX (default $HOME/.local)
    make uninstall    remove the installed binary
    make clean        remove build/


USING IT

    tam-clam -c 'echo hello; ls /'
    tam-clam script.sh
    tam-clam                  read commands from standard input

  Run with no arguments on a terminal, it prompts with "$ ". The line
  editor, history and completion arrive in 0.2.x; see ROADMAP.md.

  Before the first interactive prompt, tam-clam reads
  ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/tamlinux/tam-clam/rc if present. Define
  aliases and functions there as shell commands. Use --norc to skip the
  file for a session. Scripts, -c and piped input skip it automatically.
  Run guidance to print the keybindings and help pointers, or press
  Ctrl+G to open the line editor's guidance overlay.


WHERE THINGS ARE

  src/, include/   the shell (one module per file)
  tests/           unit tests, script tests and refusal tests
  ideas/           ideas we collect from other shells
  arch/            architecture: principles, data structures, evaluations
  keys/            keybinding specification (tam-idol standard)
  plans/           one plan per work phase (P1, P2, ...)
  bugs/            one record per bug (B1, B2, ...)
  reviews/         one record per review (R1, R2, ...)
  work/            WHATNOW (read it first: what is next) and one record
                   per work session (W1, W2, ...)
  tools/           helper scripts for developing tam-clam
  ROADMAP.md       milestones with their version numbers
  UPSTREAM.md      every piece of code we took from another project
  AI-BLAME.md      how AI work is recorded here
